#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247171 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119857 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f644bed-7adb-5907-87b4-99b1902e7850" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Space Spanned by a Set of Vectors by Identifying Pivot Columns"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04012869-2090-5170-8447-8313f2869a81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244344} {:answer/id #uuid "8115dc22-fabb-5174-ad8a-89b10ec6cb1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245454} {:answer/id #uuid "c8cb1a33-8255-5809-b2ec-783cbf2e04e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246094} {:answer/id #uuid "cc8e82d6-eab4-5a68-9f15-129539e28aca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246134} {:answer/id #uuid "d7577496-7e1a-5f47-bb20-689bea6bae6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8115dc22-fabb-5174-ad8a-89b10ec6cb1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245454} :answer-field/id #uuid "7af92bdb-0264-5972-9c25-894d9f25aaee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c69f867a-804d-5cf3-bc38-00ede80f0b28" :question/math-academy-id "q-110853" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427561 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119857 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f644bed-7adb-5907-87b4-99b1902e7850" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Space Spanned by a Set of Vectors by Identifying Pivot Columns"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5646c621-9ff4-5c1e-8500-97102b3d30e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427545} {:answer/id #uuid "830afe53-3ec3-5e7b-9c3c-099ac06955ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427546} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac2a6dd-782d-5d5d-9fa2-a488442ba8e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427547} {:answer/id #uuid "deb5edd7-a250-52b2-967e-5c46f246d2b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427553} {:answer/id #uuid "f5e5049e-edad-5162-bbb3-262144109a0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "deb5edd7-a250-52b2-967e-5c46f246d2b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427553} :answer-field/id #uuid "52b0b14b-3418-5c43-b479-6c5ffa179aac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd91b565-0528-53ef-9896-e748c3fd0d1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-75068" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],$ ${a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}],$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 16 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 16 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119858 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b72ed196-a73b-59ab-a7c3-5173249cf7e5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of a Span"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44a7260e-37d9-5ecf-b08d-1f5ce50da321" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371072} {:answer/id #uuid "03b6f1cc-ac52-5722-9030-b2f70afc1e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371073} {:answer/id #uuid "505e1e00-4bbe-58cd-93f5-9ae6fca91fb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371074} {:answer/id #uuid "fa7543da-eac6-5f26-be7d-c12dd0282c43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371075} {:answer/id #uuid "f1d79a33-33fd-5d1e-9b3e-6df988afd31c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "505e1e00-4bbe-58cd-93f5-9ae6fca91fb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371074} :answer-field/id #uuid "94fadecf-d53d-561e-b319-c2fb1000fc28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "82af09e9-f65e-5eb5-8267-28d6aa4538d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-76338" :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ ${a}_{3},$ and ${a}_{4}$ are given below. Determine whether $B={{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3},$ where ${a}_{4}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{4}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+{x}_{3}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}={{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=2 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=-4 \\\\ -9{x}_{1}-8{x}_{3}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 & -4 \\\\ -9 & 0 & -8 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 0 & -8 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+9{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 17\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 17\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=-15,$ ${x}_{2}=0,$ and ${x}_{3}=17,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n(-15)\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+0\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+17\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}={{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=(-15)+0+17=2.$"}]}
